Sjögren's Syndrome
Rheumatology & Immunology
Keratoconjunctivitis sicca, Xerostomia, Anti-Ro/SSA and Anti-La/SSB positivity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-043
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of agonizing, gritty eye pain, severe difficulty swallowing dry foods due to lack of saliva, and sudden functional decline from profound, unmanageable systemic fatigue.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient experiencing severe, unrelenting ocular pain (sensation of foreign body/corneal abrasion) and sudden inability to swallow solid food safely due to profound xerostomia. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was required to evaluate for acute corneal ulceration, rule out an acute esophageal obstruction, and provide immediate symptomatic relief and mucosal protection, as deferring care until the next in-hours period risked permanent corneal damage and severe distress.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My eyes feel like they are full of broken glass, I almost choked on a cracker because my mouth is bone dry, and I'm too exhausted to even stand."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic, progressively worsening history of a profound, persistent sensation of dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca) requiring frequent artificial tears, which have now become completely ineffective. Over the past 24 hours, the patient describes a severe, agonizing sensation of a foreign body, sand, or gravel in both eyes, severely limiting vision due to reflex tearing and pain. Concurrently, they report severe, debilitating dry mouth (xerostomia), culminating today in an episode of choking due to difficulty swallowing dry foods. Notes a history of rampant dental caries and premature tooth loss. Complains of bilateral, painless parotid gland enlargement and non-erosive, symmetrical polyarthralgias affecting the small joints of the hands. Reports profound systemic fatigue entirely out of proportion to physical findings. Vaginal dryness leading to dyspareunia is a known chronic issue.
Functional Status: Housebound; lying in a dark room due to extreme ocular discomfort; unable to eat solid meals safely.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of corneal ulceration/scarring; high risk of choking on solid foods.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, keeping eyes tightly shut, and sipping water continuously from a bottle.
Physical Exam: HEENT: Bilateral conjunctivae are markedly injected and erythematous. The corneas lack the normal glossy sheen, appearing dull (severe keratoconjunctivitis sicca); fluorescein staining (if available) would likely show punctate epithelial erosions. Oropharynx is completely desiccated; no salivary pooling is visible under the tongue. A wooden spatula sticks instantly to the buccal mucosa (positive 'cracker sign' equivalent). Bilateral parotid glands are visibly and palpably enlarged, firm, and non-tender. Musculoskeletal: Mild, symmetrical tenderness in the PIP and MCP joints of the hands without gross bogginess or deformity. Dermatological: No cutaneous vasculitis (palpable purpura) noted on the lower limbs currently. The diagnostic tetrad is highly evident: profound keratoconjunctivitis sicca and xerostomia, strongly predicting the discovery of positive anti-Ro (SSA) and anti-La (SSB) antibodies and focal lymphocytic sialadenitis on minor salivary gland biopsy.
Environmental Observations: Humidifier is not present; patient advised on environmental moisture control.
Medication Reconciliation: Using standard over-the-counter watery eye drops with zero retention or relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Sjögren's Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated (Acute severe mucosal desiccation crisis)
Differentials:

Acute Corneal Abrasion/Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The symptoms are perfectly bilateral and symmetrical, set against a chronic history of progressive dryness, rather than a unilateral acute traumatic or infectious event.

Drug-induced Anticholinergic Toxicity - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of parotid gland enlargement, symmetrical polyarthralgias, and profound chronic fatigue, which define a systemic autoimmune process rather than simple medication side effects (no recent tricyclics or antihistamines noted).

Rheumatoid Arthritis (Secondary Sjögren's) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The polyarthralgia is non-erosive and mild compared to the dominating, overwhelming presence of the primary sicca symptoms and parotid enlargement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe, acute exacerbation of primary mucosal desiccation due to Sjögren's syndrome, resulting in agonizing corneal pain and functional dysphagia. The sensation of "broken glass" in the eyes strongly suggests severe punctate keratitis or impending corneal ulceration. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was critical to immediately physically evaluate the corneas, rule out a frank infectious ulcer, and prescribe intensive, highly viscous mucosal protection. Leaving this patient unmedicated overnight carried a significant risk of irreversible corneal scarring and unmanageable, severe pain, perfectly validating the MBS 585-600 service criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe high-viscosity, preservative-free lubricating eye ointment (e.g., Paraffin-based) to be applied intensively, particularly overnight, to provide a thick mechanical barrier and prevent corneal ulceration. Prescribe an artificial saliva substitute (spray or gel) and recommend a strict soft/liquid diet to prevent choking. Arrange an urgent referral to an Ophthalmologist for a formal slit-lamp evaluation to rule out severe corneal damage, and a Rheumatologist for systemic management (e.g., secretagogues like Pilocarpine, or systemic immunosuppressants).
Positive Behavioral Change: Ocular lubrication scheduling: Apply the thick eye ointment every 2 hours and immediately before sleep; demonstrating that creating a thick, physical barrier of grease instantly stops the eyelid from scraping the raw cornea, providing immediate pain relief and reinforcing strict adherence. Dietary modification: Switch entirely to a wet/pureed diet (soups, smoothies) for the next 48 hours; proving that eliminating dry mechanical friction in the throat immediately resolves the fear and physical reality of choking.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to coordinate specialist referrals and check baseline autoimmune bloods.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or an emergency eye clinic immediately if the vision in either eye suddenly becomes highly blurred, if you develop a thick, yellow/green discharge from the eye, or if you notice a white spot forming on the colored part of your eye (the cornea).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe, painful dryness in your eyes and mouth is caused by an autoimmune attack on your moisture-producing glands. We must aggressively protect your eyes right now to prevent permanent scarring of the clear surface.
Clinical Course: The thick ointments will provide immediate physical comfort, but the underlying disease requires long-term specialist management to stimulate moisture production and protect your teeth and eyes permanently.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not rub your eyes, as this will further scratch the dry surface. If your eye suddenly becomes intensely sensitive to light, develops a thick yellow discharge, or if your vision drops significantly, you must seek emergency medical help as this could be an infection on the damaged surface.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system has destroyed the microscopic glands that make tears and saliva. Standard watery drops evaporate instantly. The thick ointment prescribed acts as an artificial, greasy shield, physically protecting your eye surface from being scratched every time you blink, giving it time to heal.
Actionable Steps: Use the thick eye ointment every 2 hours. Eat only wet, soft foods. See your GP in 2 days to organize an urgent review with an eye specialist.

